---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const updated = '2026-10-24';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology: How Every PromptDNS Tool Works"
  description="Exactly how PromptDNS tools get their answers: which DNS resolvers they query, which RFCs the parsers follow, how the domain score is computed, and what the tools cannot see."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="font-display mt-3 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">How our tools get their answers.</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-dim">
      Every check on PromptDNS runs in your browser, queries public DNS resolvers, and follows
      the published standards. This page documents exactly what that means - including what the
      tools cannot see - so you can judge the results yourself.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#where-the-dns-answers-come-from">Where the DNS answers come from</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-the-parsers-follow">What the parsers follow</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-the-domain-score-is-computed">How the domain score is computed</a></li>
        <li><a href="#known-limitations">Known limitations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-10">
      <h2 id="where-the-dns-answers-come-from">Where the DNS answers come from</h2>
      <p>
        PromptDNS tools query DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) endpoints operated by public resolvers:
        Cloudflare (1.1.1.1), Google Public DNS (8.8.8.8), and AliDNS (223.5.5.5). The queries are
        sent directly from your browser to the resolver. PromptDNS servers never see the domains
        you look up - there is no backend in the lookup path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two consequences worth knowing. First, results show what those public resolvers currently
        serve, which can differ from what your local ISP resolver serves, because of caching and
        TTLs - the propagation checker exists precisely to surface those differences. Second,
        resolvers apply their own caching, so a very recent DNS change may not be visible yet.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-the-parsers-follow">What the parsers follow</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SPF</strong> is parsed per <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RFC 7208</a>. The checker counts the DNS-querying mechanisms in your published record (<code>include:</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>mx</code>, <code>ptr</code>, <code>exists</code>, and <code>redirect=</code>) against the limit of 10 in §4.6.4. It inspects the record as published; it does not recursively expand nested includes, and it does not evaluate a sent message - that is receiver-side behaviour we cannot observe from a record.</li>
        <li><strong>DMARC</strong> is parsed per <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7489" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RFC 7489</a>: policy (<code>p=</code>), subdomain policy, aggregate/forensic reporting addresses, alignment mode, and <code>pct</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>DKIM</strong> record structure follows <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6376" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RFC 6376</a>. Because selectors are chosen by each sender, DKIM checks query the common selectors your provider documents and report what is found - a missing selector we didn't try is not proof DKIM is absent.</li>
        <li><strong>MTA-STS and TLS-RPT</strong> checks follow <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8461" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RFC 8461</a> and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8460" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RFC 8460</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Blacklist checks</strong> query public DNSBL zones directly from your browser for the IP or domain you enter. Being listed on a zone we don't query is invisible to us; we list which zones each check covers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="how-the-domain-score-is-computed">How the domain score is computed</h2>
      <p>
        The homepage scanner runs seven checks and produces a weighted score. Each check earns a
        portion of its weight:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Weight</th><th>What earns the points</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>DMARC</td><td>25</td><td>Record exists; fuller points for an enforced policy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SPF</td><td>20</td><td>Valid record within the 10-lookup limit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Website DNS</td><td>15</td><td>A/AAAA records resolve for the apex</td></tr>
            <tr><td>DKIM</td><td>15</td><td>Selector records found for common selectors</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mail Routing (MX)</td><td>10</td><td>MX records exist and point to distinct hosts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>DNSSEC</td><td>10</td><td>DNSKEY/DS chain present</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Nameservers</td><td>5</td><td>Delegated NS records resolve</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4">
        The score is the earned weight divided by total weight (100). Grades: A ≥ 90,
        B ≥ 75, C ≥ 60, D ≥ 40, F below 40. The score is deterministic - the same DNS state
        always produces the same score - and says nothing about mail <em>reputation</em>, only
        about the DNS state the checks can observe.
      </p>

      <h2 id="known-limitations">Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Lookups run from your browser, so geo-specific answers (GeoDNS, split-horizon) reflect the resolvers' vantage points, not every region.</li>
        <li>Record checkers inspect published DNS only. They do not send test messages, so they cannot tell you how a specific receiver will score your mail.</li>
        <li>Tools that fetch pages (robots.txt, headers, AI-crawler checks) are subject to CORS restrictions and use public CORS relays as a fallback when a site blocks direct browser reads; results can lag or fail when relays are down, and the tools say so instead of guessing.</li>
        <li>Anything time-sensitive (propagation, TTL) is a snapshot, not a guarantee about future resolver state.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a result looks wrong, tell us the domain, the record type, and what you expected at
        <a href="mailto:bugs@promptdns.com" class="text-accent">bugs@promptdns.com</a>. Notable
        corrections are recorded on the <a href="/changelog/" class="text-accent">changelog</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  
    <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">DNS propagation checker</a>, the multi-resolver method in practice</li>
        <li><a href="/email-header-analyzer/">Email header analyzer</a>, the local parsing example from this page</li>
        <li><a href="/how-it-works/">How PromptDNS works</a>, the product walkthrough</li>
      </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
